{
  num: 3,
  file: 'Episode 3 - Quadratic Number Patterns',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA', t: `Episode three. Quadratic number patterns.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `So far we have had two kinds of pattern. One where you add the same amount. One where you multiply by the same amount.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `And today it is neither.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Today it is neither. Today the gaps between the numbers keep changing. And the exam loves this one, because it looks impossible until you know the trick.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Before that, you owe me an answer.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `I do. Last time the sequence was sixteen, four, one. Which term is equal to one over one thousand and twenty four?` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `I kept dividing by four. Sixteen, four, one, a quarter, a sixteenth, and so on. I landed on the eighth term.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `The eighth term. Correct. Now let us meet today's pattern.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Same paper as the last two episodes. November two thousand and fourteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `The sequence is negative one, negative seven, negative eleven, and then a term called p.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `First question. Write down the value of p.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Easy. Negative one to negative seven is minus six. So I take another six off. Negative eleven minus six is negative seventeen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Check the second gap before you commit.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Negative seven to negative eleven. That is minus four.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Oh. The gaps are not the same. Minus six, then minus four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `So it is not arithmetic. And you cannot use last week's formula either, because we are not multiplying. This is the moment most learners get stuck.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Then how do I find the next gap?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `You do the same thing again. You look at the gaps between the gaps.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `The gaps have an exam name. They are called first differences. Later term minus earlier term, exactly like before.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Our first differences are negative six, and negative four.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Now subtract those. Negative four minus negative six.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Minus a negative, so that is negative four plus six. That gives two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Two. And that is called the second difference. When the second difference stays the same, the pattern is called a quadratic sequence.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `So arithmetic is a constant first difference, and quadratic is a constant second difference.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `That is the cleanest way to hold it in your head.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `So now find p. Your first differences were negative six, negative four. What comes next?` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `They go up by two each time. So the next one is negative two.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Then p is negative eleven, plus negative two. That is negative thirteen.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Two marks. And notice your first answer, negative seventeen, was wrong only because you assumed the gap stayed the same. Always check the second gap.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Next question. Determine the enth term. Four marks.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Every quadratic sequence has the same shape. The enth term equals a, n squared, plus b n, plus c. You just need the three letters.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Three facts give you the three letters. First, two a equals the second difference.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Our second difference is two. So two a is two, and a is one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Second, three a plus b equals the first difference. The very first one.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Three times one, plus b, equals negative six. So b is negative nine.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Third, a plus b plus c equals the first term.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `One, plus negative nine, plus c, equals negative one. So c is seven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Put them together.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `The enth term equals n squared, minus nine n, plus seven.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Now test it, always. Put n equals three.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Nine, minus twenty seven, plus seven. That is negative eleven. And that is the third term.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Last question, and this is where the marks are won. The first difference between two terms next to each other is ninety six. Find those two terms.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `That sounds far away.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `It is. But look at the first differences again. Negative six, negative four, negative two. What kind of sequence is that?` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `They go up by two every time. That is arithmetic.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `So use episode one on the differences themselves. First term negative six, common difference two. But we need a new letter. n is already the term number of our main sequence, and now we are counting differences instead. So let us call the difference number k.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `So the kth difference is negative six, plus k minus one, times two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Set that equal to ninety six.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Two k minus eight equals ninety six. So two k is one hundred and four, and k is fifty two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `So the gap of ninety six sits between the fifty second term and the fifty third. Now use your rule.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Fifty two squared is two thousand seven hundred and four. Minus nine times fifty two, which is four hundred and sixty eight. Plus seven. That gives two thousand two hundred and forty three.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `And the next one. Fifty three squared is two thousand eight hundred and nine, minus four hundred and seventy seven, plus seven. Two thousand three hundred and thirty nine.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Subtract them and check.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Two thousand three hundred and thirty nine, minus two thousand two hundred and forty three, is ninety six.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Four things.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Number one. Always check the second gap before you decide a pattern is arithmetic. That single check would have saved Naledi's first answer.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Number two. Subtracting a negative adds. Negative four minus negative six is positive two. This is where most of the lost marks live.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Number three. Two a equals the second difference, not a on its own. Forgetting to halve it is very common.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Number four. Test your rule on a term you already know before you use it on a big one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Three things to remember.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `One. If the first differences are not equal, take the differences again.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Two. A constant second difference means the pattern is quadratic.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Three. The shape is always a n squared plus b n plus c, and three facts unlock it. Two a is the second difference. Three a plus b is the first difference. A plus b plus c is the first term.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Here is your question. Same sequence. Our rule was n squared, minus nine n, plus seven. Which term is equal to seventeen?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Pause and try it. I will wait.`, pause: 3.0 },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `I will give you the answer next time. And next time we stop looking at single terms, and we start adding them all up.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Adding up hundreds of numbers sounds worse, not better.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `It sounds worse. It is actually easier, and there is a symbol that says do it. That is episode four.` },
  ],
  slides: [
    { n: 1, afterSeg: 0, title: `EPISODE 3 • QUADRATIC NUMBER PATTERNS`, onScreen: `EPISODE 3 — QUADRATIC NUMBER PATTERNS` },
    { n: 2, afterSeg: 1, title: `ADD VERSUS MULTIPLY`, onScreen: `ADD the same amount · MULTIPLY by the same amount` },
    { n: 3, afterSeg: 3, title: `THE GAPS KEEP CHANGING`, onScreen: `TODAY — the gaps keep changing` },
    { n: 4, afterSeg: 5, title: `LAST TIME • WHICH TERM IS ONE OVER 1024`, onScreen: `16 ; 4 ; 1 ; … Which term equals 1/1024?` },
    { n: 5, afterSeg: 6, title: `ANSWER • THE 8TH TERM`, onScreen: `T₈ = 1/1024` },
    { n: 6, afterSeg: 8, title: `NSC PAPER 1 • NOVEMBER 2014`, onScreen: `NSC Mathematics P1 · November 2014 · Question 3.1` },
    { n: 7, afterSeg: 9, title: `THE SEQUENCE`, onScreen: `−1 ; −7 ; −11 ; p ; …` },
    { n: 8, afterSeg: 10, title: `THE QUESTION • FIND P`, onScreen: `3.1.1 Write down the value of p. (2)` },
    { n: 9, afterSeg: 11, title: `THE WRONG ASSUMPTION`, onScreen: `Naledi's answer: p = −17` },
    { n: 10, afterSeg: 13, title: `THE TWO GAPS`, onScreen: `−1 → −7 is −6 · −7 → −11 is −4` },
    { n: 11, afterSeg: 14, title: `NOT ARITHMETIC`, onScreen: `the gaps are NOT equal → not arithmetic ✗` },
    { n: 12, afterSeg: 17, title: `GAPS BETWEEN THE GAPS`, onScreen: `look at the gaps between the gaps` },
    { n: 13, afterSeg: 18, title: `FIRST DIFFERENCES`, onScreen: `FIRST DIFFERENCES — later term minus earlier term` },
    { n: 14, afterSeg: 19, title: `OUR FIRST DIFFERENCES`, onScreen: `first differences: −6 ; −4` },
    { n: 15, afterSeg: 21, title: `DIFFERENCE THEM AGAIN`, onScreen: `−4 − (−6) = 2` },
    { n: 16, afterSeg: 22, title: `SECOND DIFFERENCE IS 2`, onScreen: `SECOND DIFFERENCE = 2 · constant second difference → QUADRATIC SEQUENCE` },
    { n: 17, afterSeg: 24, title: `ARITHMETIC VERSUS QUADRATIC`, onScreen: `arithmetic → 1st difference constant · quadratic → 2nd difference constant` },
    { n: 18, afterSeg: 26, title: `THE NEXT FIRST DIFFERENCE`, onScreen: `first differences: −6 ; −4 ; −2` },
    { n: 19, afterSeg: 27, title: `ANSWER • P IS −13`, onScreen: `p = −11 + (−2) = −13 ✓` },
    { n: 20, afterSeg: 29, title: `THE QUESTION • NTH TERM`, onScreen: `3.1.2 Determine the nth term of the sequence. (4)` },
    { n: 21, afterSeg: 30, title: `THE QUADRATIC SHAPE`, onScreen: `Tₙ = an² + bn + c` },
    { n: 22, afterSeg: 31, title: `FACT 1 • TWO A`, onScreen: `2a = second difference` },
    { n: 23, afterSeg: 32, title: `SOLVE FOR A`, onScreen: `2a = 2 → a = 1` },
    { n: 24, afterSeg: 33, title: `FACT 2 • THREE A PLUS B`, onScreen: `3a + b = first difference` },
    { n: 25, afterSeg: 34, title: `SOLVE FOR B`, onScreen: `3(1) + b = −6 → b = −9` },
    { n: 26, afterSeg: 35, title: `FACT 3 • A PLUS B PLUS C`, onScreen: `a + b + c = T₁` },
    { n: 27, afterSeg: 36, title: `SOLVE FOR C`, onScreen: `1 + (−9) + c = −1 → c = 7` },
    { n: 28, afterSeg: 38, title: `ANSWER • THE NTH TERM`, onScreen: `Tₙ = n² − 9n + 7 ✓ (boxed)` },
    { n: 29, afterSeg: 40, title: `TEST THE RULE`, onScreen: `n = 3 → 9 − 27 + 7 = −11 ✓` },
    { n: 30, afterSeg: 41, title: `THE QUESTION • DIFFERENCE OF 96`, onScreen: `3.1.3 The first difference between two consecutive terms is 96. Calculate these two terms. (4)` },
    { n: 31, afterSeg: 44, title: `FIRST DIFFERENCES ARE ARITHMETIC`, onScreen: `first differences: −6 ; −4 ; −2 ; … an ARITHMETIC sequence` },
    { n: 32, afterSeg: 46, title: `THE KTH DIFFERENCE`, onScreen: `−6 + (k − 1)(2) = 2k − 8` },
    { n: 33, afterSeg: 48, title: `SOLVE FOR K`, onScreen: `2k − 8 = 96 → k = 52` },
    { n: 34, afterSeg: 50, title: `THE 52ND TERM`, onScreen: `T₅₂ = 2 704 − 468 + 7 = 2 243` },
    { n: 35, afterSeg: 51, title: `THE 53RD TERM`, onScreen: `T₅₃ = 2 809 − 477 + 7 = 2 339` },
    { n: 36, afterSeg: 53, title: `CHECK • DIFFERENCE IS 96`, onScreen: `2 339 − 2 243 = 96 ✓` },
    { n: 37, afterSeg: 54, title: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS`, onScreen: `WHERE YOU LOSE MARKS` },
    { n: 38, afterSeg: 55, title: `MARK LOSS 1 • CHECK THE SECOND GAP`, onScreen: `1. check the second gap first` },
    { n: 39, afterSeg: 56, title: `MARK LOSS 2 • MINUS A NEGATIVE`, onScreen: `2. − (−6) means + 6` },
    { n: 40, afterSeg: 57, title: `MARK LOSS 3 • HALVE IT`, onScreen: `3. 2a = second difference, so halve it` },
    { n: 41, afterSeg: 58, title: `MARK LOSS 4 • TEST THE RULE`, onScreen: `4. always test the rule first` },
    { n: 42, afterSeg: 59, title: `REMEMBER`, onScreen: `REMEMBER` },
    { n: 43, afterSeg: 60, title: `RECAP 1 • DIFFERENCE AGAIN`, onScreen: `1. differences not equal → difference them again` },
    { n: 44, afterSeg: 61, title: `RECAP 2 • QUADRATIC`, onScreen: `2. constant second difference = quadratic` },
    { n: 45, afterSeg: 62, title: `RECAP 3 • THE THREE FACTS`, onScreen: `3. 2a = 2nd difference · 3a + b = 1st difference · a + b + c = T₁` },
    { n: 46, afterSeg: 63, title: `YOUR TURN • WHICH TERM IS 17`, onScreen: `Tₙ = n² − 9n + 7. Which term equals 17?` },
    { n: 47, afterSeg: 64, title: `PAUSE AND TRY`, onScreen: `PAUSE AND TRY` },
