{
  num: 4,
  file: 'Episode 4 - Series and Sigma Notation',
  segs: [
    { s: 'AMELIA', t: `Episode four. Series and sigma notation.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Until now we have been finding single terms. The twelfth one. The two hundred and fifty first one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Today we stop looking at one term, and we start adding them all together.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `And there is a symbol for that.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `There is one symbol that says, add all of these up. It scares people. By the end of today it will not scare you.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `But first, my answer.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Last time our rule was n squared, minus nine n, plus seven. Which term is equal to seventeen?` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `I made it equal to seventeen, then made one side zero. So n squared, minus nine n, minus ten, equals zero.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `And factorise.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `n minus ten, times n plus one. So n is ten, or n is negative one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Now be careful. Which one do you keep?` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Ten. There is no term number negative one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `The tenth term. And saying why you reject the negative one is itself worth a mark. Do not just cross it out silently.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Now, two words that sound the same and are not. Sequence, and series.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `A sequence is a list. Two, semicolon, four, semicolon, six, semicolon, eight.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `A series is the same numbers, added together. Two plus four plus six plus eight.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `So the semicolons become plus signs.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `That is the whole difference. A list, or a total. And look back at the very first episode. Our exam question was written with plus signs all along.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Writing out two hundred and fifty one numbers with plus signs is not practical. So we use a symbol. It is the Greek letter sigma, and it simply means, add these up.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Sigma has three parts. Underneath, where the counting starts. On top, where the counting stops. And on the right, the rule that makes each term.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `So it is like instructions. Start here, stop there, and this is what to add.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Exactly that. Let us read one together.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Here is a small one. Sigma, from k equals one, to k equals four, of three k.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Start at one. Put one into three k.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Three.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Then two, then three, then four.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Six. Nine. Twelve.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `And add them.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Thirty.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `So sigma is not difficult. It is a short way of writing a long addition.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Now the real one. Same paper, same series as episode one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Two plus nine plus sixteen, and so on, for two hundred and fifty one terms. Express this series in sigma notation.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Do I have to find a new rule?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `You already have it. We found it in episode one.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Seven n minus five.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `So I write sigma, from n equals one, on top the last term, which was one thousand seven hundred and fifty two.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Stop there. What does the number on top count?` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `The … terms?` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `The terms. Not the value of the last term. The top number tells you how many times to count, not what the last answer is.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `So it is two hundred and fifty one, because there are two hundred and fifty one terms.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Two hundred and fifty one. This one mistake costs marks in every single exam.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `So build it. Start value underneath.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `n equals one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Stop value on top.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Two hundred and fifty one.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `And the rule on the right.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Seven n minus five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Put it together and read it out loud.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Sigma, from n equals one, to two hundred and fifty one, of seven n minus five.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Two marks. And test it, the same way we test everything. Put n equals one into the rule.` },
    { s: 'NALEDI', t: `Seven minus five is two. And two is the first term.` },
    { s: 'MICHAEL', t: `Put n equals two.` },
